    "done_when": "tests/test_control_is_compared_with_the_head_arm.py gains three cases, each building a manifest plus logs in a temporary run directory and calling the gate's control check: an after_suite with no completed key, one with completed null, one with completed true. The first two fall back to the baseline comparison (a control whose failing id the baseline also fails is refused), while completed true keeps the head-arm decision. The missing-key case fails at the base revision because the head arm decides it (logged) and passes at the head. The negative control, in a scratch copy, restores the literal-False-only check; the missing-key case fails, with the mutation on the red log's first line and an EXIT= line after pytest's output. The gate is that test file plus every tests/ file whose name contains negative_control or control_gate, run with -n 8 --timeout=300; both arms are recorded as one JSON object each under baseline_suite and after_suite (revision, command, exit_status, one log_path, failure_ids as a JSON list), each log ending in an EXIT= line, with the head arm adding no failing id. ruff check plus ruff format --check on the changed paths add no finding against the base.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Plan crew-runtime-positive-controls, followup f-crpc-a-head-arm-without-completed-is-unreadable: a head arm that omits its completed key does not decide a negative control. Read the followup in docs/plans/crew-runtime-positive-controls.html. The head-arm reader in reckon/crew/promotion.py (near :4870, added by control-is-compared-with-the-head-arm at 725632ca) treats only a literal completed: False as unreadable, so an after_suite that omits completed or carries null is read as a completed arm, deciding the control, while the same mapping would be refused on the baseline side. Treat a missing or null completed as unreadable on both arms, so the baseline comparison decides when the head arm is not known to have completed.",
